TASK
1. Decompose the proposed product into between {{ min_modules }} and {{ max_modules }} MODULES — the real load-bearing parts of the system, each one a thing this business would recognize as a distinct piece of how they operate (e.g. "Intake & identification portal", "Session scheduling engine", "Insurance pre-approval tracker" — never generic filler like "Dashboard module"). Choose the number this business actually needs; do not pad to the maximum.
2. Build the BUSINESS CASE: exactly how this product makes or protects money for THIS business. Ground it in how they earn today.

Number rules — absolute:
- If owner numbers exist above: every figure you write must be one of those numbers or plain arithmetic on them, and must name the input it came from ("at your $85 average visit...", "of the 14 hours you told us about..."). State every assumption you introduce and make it conservative ("if reminders recover even a third of the no-shows"). Put the computed figures in payback_logic and in the relevant costs_removed / revenue_streams entries, and close each computed claim with "by your own figures".
- DO THE ARITHMETIC. When two of their numbers combine into a third (a volume × a price, a rate × a capacity, hours × a frequency), compute it and write the RESULT as a number — that computed number is the consulting. Whenever two combinable inputs exist, payback_logic must contain at least one multiplied-out figure; restating inputs without computing ("a new center every 2 weeks will contribute revenue") is a failure — the standard is the product, written out ("a new center every 2 weeks is ~2 a month: each onboarding month adds ~$1,800 of recurring revenue at your planned price, by your own figures").
{% if operating_stage == "opening" %}- This business has NOT launched: their numbers are targets and plans, not history. Compute forward from the plan and label it as their plan ("at your planned $30 ticket..."). Never predict that a target will be hit — frame value as capacity, readiness and avoided hires, not projected profits.
{% endif %}- Where no owner number covers a claim: describe the mechanism only. Never invent currency amounts or percentages — a made-up number is worse than none.

